From: CARGT <engineering@cargt.com>
Subject: [PATCH] init-resize: skip userfs resize if already done

This script's own "already resized" guard (a marker file at
$ROOTFS_DIR/etc/.resized) lives inside whichever rootfs is currently
booting, gating the *entire* loop over userfs/vendorfs/bootfs/rootfs -
so on a RAUC A/B board it doesn't protect userfs the way it's meant
to: rootfs/vendorfs/bootfs are already fixed-size A/B slots skipped
unconditionally (see 0002), but userfs is a single partition shared
across both banks, and a fresh rootfs bank has never had a chance to
create /etc/.resized. The result: every OTA update's first boot into
the newly-installed bank re-runs a full e2fsck -c bad-blocks scan
(plus resize2fs) against userfs, even though userfs itself was
already grown to its full size on the very first boot, long before
this bank existed.

Confirmed on hardware (stm32mp25-cargt-00395-01-00365): this full scan
against a ~22GB userfs took minutes - far longer than OP-TEE's own
watchdog (stm32_iwdg) tolerates before it panics and resets the SoC.
Since this happens in the resize initramfs, before the kernel proper
or systemd-journald ever starts, the reset left no journal or pstore
trace - it looked identical to an unexplained early-boot hang. It
recurred on every boot attempt into the freshly-installed bank
(3 times, exhausting bootcount) before U-Boot's rollback-safety logic
correctly fell back to the previous good bank.

resize-helper.service (the later, systemd-stage resize path) already
solved this exact problem for itself with a marker file resident on
userfs (/usr/local/.resize-helper-done - see resize-helper's own
0003-skip-userfs-resize-if-already-done.patch), which survives an OTA
update to either rootfs bank because it lives on the shared partition,
not inside rootfs. Give init-resize.sh's userfs case the same
protection: mount the userfs device long enough to check for that
marker (the same relative path, since /usr/local is exactly where
userfs is later mounted), skip the fsck+resize entirely if present,
and write the marker on userfs after resizing it here so a fresh
board's very first boot doesn't rely on resize-helper.service running
afterward to do it - either script can now set (and both respect) the
one shared marker.

Upstream-Status: Pending [not sent upstream to ST - specific to a
custom A/B partition scheme]

--- a/init-resize.sh
+++ b/init-resize.sh
@@ -40,22 +40,68 @@
                             if [ $? -eq 0 ]; then
                                 case $label in
                                 user*)
-                                    echo "RESIZE USERFS [$DEVICE]"
-                                    # A single e2fsck pass isn't enough on a
-                                    # never-before-checked filesystem: when it
-                                    # actually fixes something (common on a
-                                    # fresh image's very first real check,
-                                    # reported as "FILE SYSTEM WAS MODIFIED"),
-                                    # resize2fs refuses to run against it
-                                    # ("Please run 'e2fsck -f' first") even
-                                    # though e2fsck just ran - it wants a
-                                    # subsequent CLEAN pass before trusting
-                                    # the filesystem enough to resize it. Run
-                                    # a second, fast recheck (no -c bad-blocks
-                                    # rescan needed) to satisfy that.
-                                    /sbin/e2fsck -f -y -c -C 0 $DEVICE
-                                    /sbin/e2fsck -f -y -C 0 $DEVICE
-                                    /sbin/resize2fs $DEVICE
+                                    # userfs is shared across both RAUC A/B
+                                    # rootfs banks, but the $ROOTFS_DIR/etc/
+                                    # .resized marker gating this whole loop
+                                    # lives inside whichever rootfs is
+                                    # currently booting - a freshly OTA-
+                                    # installed bank has never set it, so
+                                    # without its own check userfs's full
+                                    # e2fsck -c bad-blocks scan re-runs on
+                                    # every OTA update's first boot into the
+                                    # new bank, even though userfs was
+                                    # already grown on the very first boot
+                                    # long before that bank existed. Check
+                                    # the same marker resize-helper.service
+                                    # uses post-boot (a file resident on
+                                    # userfs itself, so it survives an OTA
+                                    # update to either bank) before doing
+                                    # any of this.
+                                    userfs_already_resized=0
+                                    mkdir -p /tmp/userfs-check
+                                    if mount -o ro $DEVICE /tmp/userfs-check 2> /dev/null; then
+                                        [ -f /tmp/userfs-check/.resize-helper-done ] && userfs_already_resized=1
+                                        umount /tmp/userfs-check
+                                    fi
+                                    rmdir /tmp/userfs-check 2> /dev/null
+                                    if [ "$userfs_already_resized" = "1" ]; then
+                                        echo "SKIP USERFS [$DEVICE] (already resized)"
+                                    else
+                                        echo "RESIZE USERFS [$DEVICE]"
+                                        # No -c (bad-blocks) pass: it does a full
+                                        # raw read of every sector, which on a
+                                        # truly virgin, full-size userfs (~22-26GB)
+                                        # takes minutes - long enough that OP-TEE's
+                                        # own watchdog (stm32_iwdg) panics and
+                                        # resets the SoC before it finishes, since
+                                        # nothing feeds that watchdog this early in
+                                        # boot (confirmed on hardware: a real reset
+                                        # loop on first boot into a freshly-flashed
+                                        # board, stuck at ~2.68% after 8s - a full
+                                        # pass would take ~5 minutes). eMMC/SD flash
+                                        # remaps bad physical blocks internally via
+                                        # its own FTL and doesn't expose them to the
+                                        # OS the way a rotational disk would, so this
+                                        # check has little practical value here
+                                        # anyway. A single fast consistency-only
+                                        # pass isn't enough on a never-before-checked
+                                        # filesystem: when it actually fixes something
+                                        # (common on a fresh image's very first real
+                                        # check, reported as "FILE SYSTEM WAS
+                                        # MODIFIED"), resize2fs refuses to run against
+                                        # it ("Please run 'e2fsck -f' first") even
+                                        # though e2fsck just ran - it wants a
+                                        # subsequent clean pass before trusting the
+                                        # filesystem enough to resize it. Run it twice.
+                                        /sbin/e2fsck -f -y -C 0 $DEVICE
+                                        /sbin/e2fsck -f -y -C 0 $DEVICE
+                                        /sbin/resize2fs $DEVICE
+                                        mkdir -p /tmp/userfs-mark
+                                        mount $DEVICE /tmp/userfs-mark
+                                        touch /tmp/userfs-mark/.resize-helper-done
+                                        umount /tmp/userfs-mark
+                                        rmdir /tmp/userfs-mark
+                                    fi
                                     ;;
                                 root*)
                                     echo "SKIP ROOTFS [$DEVICE] (fixed-size A/B slot)"
